{"from": "text:strabo-geography-14-1", "type": "attests", "to": "place:trogyllium", "grade": "A", "note": "Strabo names 'the promontory called Trogilian' and 'the Trogilian promontory itself is a kind of spur of Mt. Mycale' (14.1.20) - the same cape Acts 20:15 anchors at, opposite Samos."}
{"from": "text:strabo-geography-14-1", "type": "attests", "to": "place:samos", "grade": "A", "note": "Strabo names Samos, its position facing south 'both it and its harbor', and the strait between Mycale and Samos (14.1.20)."}
{"from": "text:strabo-geography-14-1", "type": "attests", "to": "place:chios", "grade": "A", "note": "Strabo names Chios in the Ionian seaboard sequence 'as far as Phocaea and Chios and Samos' (14.1.3) - the island Acts 20:15 passes."}
{"from": "text:strabo-geography-14-1", "type": "attests", "to": "place:miletus", "grade": "A", "note": "Strabo names Miletus repeatedly and begins the bounds of the Ionian coast 'from the Poseidium of the Milesians' (14.1.2-7); the oracle of Apollo Didymeus is 'eighteen stadia inland' from it."}
{"from": "observation:trogyllium-samos-distance", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-15-itinerary", "grade": "B", "note": "Strabo's forty stadia (about 7.2 km) between the Trogilian promontory and Samos confirms the close island-hopping geography of the 20:15 leg ('touched at Samos and stayed at Trogyllium'); it attests the setting, not the voyage."}
{"from": "artifact:thessalonica-politarch-aristarchus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-04-aristarchus-thessalonian", "grade": "D", "note": "The inscription (IG X,2 1 30) names 'Aristarchus son of Aristarchus, a politarch of Thessalonica' - the same city and name as Paul's companion; but its date (39/38 BC?) is decades before Acts and the identification is only conjectured (Wikipedia)."}
{"from": "artifact:herodian-oil-lamp", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-08-upper-room-lamps", "grade": "C", "note": "The Herodian clay oil lamp, the common 1st-century household lamp - the kind of light that filled an upper room at night; a Judean type, so context only for a Troas meeting."}
{"from": "text:pliny-epistles-10-96", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-07-first-day-breaking-bread", "grade": "C", "note": "Pliny reports Bithynian Christians meeting 'on a certain day before light' and sharing a meal - the fixed-day assembly with a common meal, a generation after Acts and in the province next to Asia; not a Troas record."}
{"from": "site:miletus", "type": "attests", "to": "place:miletus", "grade": "A", "note": "The excavated city of Miletus - excavated since 1873 (Rayet, then Huelsen and Wiegand), with the Market Gate reassembled in Berlin - is the port where Paul's ship put in and the Ephesian elders met him."}
{"from": "artifact:miletus-godfearers-inscription", "type": "found_at", "to": "site:miletus"}
