{"from": "claim:ac-20-09-12-eutychus-raised", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-acts-20"}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-05-06-troas-seven-days", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-16-08-troas", "note": "Second visit to Troas; the first visit (three days) was on the second journey, Acts 16:8-11."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-06-after-unleavened", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-12-03-04-unleavened-passover", "note": "The same festival calendar: Acts dates events to the days of unleavened bread / Passover week in 12:3-4 and 20:6."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-06-after-unleavened", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-14-12-sacrificed-passover", "note": "Mark 14:12 uses 'the first day of unleavened bread, when they sacrificed the Passover'; Acts 20:6 dates a departure to the end of the same festival week."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-02-03-greece-three-months", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-19-21-22-journey-plan", "note": "Acts 19:21 announced the plan to go through Macedonia and Achaia to Jerusalem; 20:1-3 executes it."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-04-companions", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-19-29-gaius-aristarchus", "note": "Aristarchus (and a man named Gaius) seized at Ephesus in 19:29 reappear in the 20:4 list; whether the Gaius of 19:29 'of Macedonia' is the Gaius of Derbe here is disputed."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-16-past-ephesus-pentecost", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-02-01-pentecost-feast", "note": "Pentecost, the feast fifty days after Passover (Acts 2:1), is used in 20:16 as a travel deadline; the feast's calendar place is the shared fact."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-31-three-years-ephesus", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-19-09-10-school-of-tyrannus", "note": "Acts 19:8-10 reports three months in the synagogue and two years in the hall of Tyrannus; 20:31 rounds the whole Ephesian ministry to three years."}
{"from": "claim:ac-20-04-companions", "type": "related_to", "to": "artifact:temple-warning-inscription", "note": "Trophimus of Asia reappears in Acts 21:29 ('Trophimus the Ephesian'), whose supposed temple entry the mob charged against Paul; the temple-warning inscription (already in the graph) attests the Gentile-exclusion setting of that later verse, not this list."}
{"from": "artifact:elephantine-passover-letter", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-06-after-unleavened", "grade": "C", "note": "The Elephantine Passover Letter of 419 BCE instructs the garrison on observing the Festival of Unleavened Bread - the 'days of unleavened bread' as a defined observance period; centuries earlier and Judeo-Egyptian, so context only (same treatment as claim:ac-12-03-04-unleavened-passover)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-06-after-unleavened", "grade": "C", "note": "Pesachim 5:1 fixes the Temple's festival schedule for the Passover eve - the festival week whose close dates the departure of 20:6. Compiled c. 200 CE; may not describe mid-first-century practice exactly (same treatment as claim:ac-12-03-04-unleavened-passover)."}
{"from": "site:miletus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-17-elders-miletus", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated port of Miletus, whose harbour silted and whose neighbours Priene and Myus had already lost theirs, is the roadstead where the ship waited while the elders came out from Ephesus (about 40 miles); it attests the setting, not the summons."}
{"from": "artifact:miletus-godfearers-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-20-17-elders-miletus", "grade": "C", "note": "The reused theatre-seat inscription (CIJ 748) names Jews and God-fearers at Miletus - the living, inscribed city of the Roman imperial period where the meeting took place; later than Acts, and not about the elders."}
{"from": "text:strabo-geography-14-1", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:perseus-strabo-14-1-trogilian"}
